Z||000000||artherd||11-26-2001||03:54 AM||||Summit sells both, which are the better product?<P>(have to consider both the Al ends, and the tubing itself.  Aeromotive seems to make better tubing, yes?)<P>(and where should I order them from, other than Summit? Pricy little buggers after you get done deciding how many you need where  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000001||theogre||11-26-2001||07:45 PM||||You may already know this...<P>If you are going that route, buy the aluminum wrenches for the AN fittings or you'll tear the hell out of the anodized finish really easily.<P>Conversions to AN style lines is expensive no matter how you do it. If you have any millitary surplus near by, see if there's anything there you can use. You may be able to find prefabbed ridged lines you can use as well.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||artherd||11-26-2001||08:07 PM||||thanks, yeah, you do need to use the special Al wrenches.<P>Yeah, I want to go AN fittings for at least the fuel lines (these would be good things NOT to leak!) and the clutch hydraulics as well (all new/custom, won't be a stock fiero part left actually. converting to a hydraulic TO bearing, and Wilwood master cyl.)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000003||lowCG||11-26-2001||08:12 PM||||Ben,you might try Baker Precision for those parts as well,good selection.||||reg||1
Z||000004||artherd||11-26-2001||11:22 PM||||Thanks LowCG, almost forgot about those guys! <BR>Hrm, the 'Speedflow' brand actually looks to be the best part (design wise, single-piece consturction, AND the cutter type hose attachment.)  Of course, they say "if you have to ask..." about the price...   <P>Then again, I'd just assume my fuel lines NOT leak and explode and kill me, so hey ;)<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000005||watts||11-27-2001||01:02 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>Yeah, I want to go AN fittings for at least the fuel lines </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Keep in mind that true AN fittings are 37 degree's at the tip, and 'farm' flare fittings are 42 degrees (someone correct me on the farm fittings if I'm wrong).<P>Anodized blue ones are aluminum, black ones are steel.<P>If you're not putting a heap of pressure on them (like for hydraulics) you can use a 42 with a 37 fitting...  but...  uh...  do so at your own risk.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>87/88 V6 5spd Notchback Hybrid<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/watts_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
